<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kircholm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-swedish-war-1600-1611">Polish–Swedish War (1600–1611)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kircholm was fought in 1605 near Riga between the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and a larger Swedish army. Commonwealth forces under Jan Karol Chodkiewicz, led by the winged hussar cavalry, won a crushing victory. It is regarded as one of the greatest successes of the Commonwealth's army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kircholm.jpg" alt="Battle of Kircholm" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Snayers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pieter_Snayers_-_Battle_of_Kircholm,_1605.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle took place on 27 September 1605 (17 September in the old style) near Kircholm, now Salaspils in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, about 18 kilometres south-east of Riga, during the Polish-Swedish War of 1600 to 1611. A Swedish expedition under Charles IX was besieging Riga. His force had 10,868 men and 11 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, and included two western commanders, Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a> and Count Joachim Frederick of Mansfeld, with a few thousand German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> mercenaries. The Polish Crown declined to raise funds or send troops, so the relief army of Jan Karol Chodkiewicz, Great Hetman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, was tired and hungry. He promised to pay the wages from his own fortune, which brought an influx of recruits.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Chodkiewicz had about 1,000 infantry, 2,600 cavalry and five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, a disadvantage of roughly one to three. His cavalry were mostly superbly trained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-hussars">winged hussars</a> armed with lances, while the Swedish cavalry carried pistols and carbines on poorer horses and were tired after a night march in torrential rain. The Swedes stood in four lines on a ridge, the infantry in squares of 30 by 30. Chodkiewicz skirmished for hours, then feigned a withdrawal, and the Swedes advanced to the foot of the slope.</p><p>The hussars charged: about 1,000 shattered Mansfeld's reiters on the Swedish right, and 650 under Jan Piotr Sapieha attacked on the left. After Charles committed his reserve of 700 cavalry, Chodkiewicz committed his own. Within 30 minutes the Swedish cavalry was in full retreat, exposing the infantry. The Swedes lost at least 5,000 killed and 500 captured, perhaps up to 8,000 killed, captured or dispersed; the Commonwealth lost about 100 dead and 200 wounded. Charles IX's horse was shot under him, and the officer Henrik Wrede gave the king his own and was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Charles IX abandoned the siege of Riga and withdrew by sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, giving up control of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. The Commonwealth could not exploit the victory, because its troops had not been paid for months and many trained horses had been lost. A truce was signed in 1611, war resumed in 1617, and in 1621 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> landed near Riga and took the city after a brief siege, which in Swedish eyes wiped away much of the shame of Kircholm. The battle is commemorated on the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kircholm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Kircholm</b> in 1605, a Commonwealth force of some 3,600 men, its striking power provided by hussars, routed a Swedish army of around 11,000 in a short and decisive engagement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1600 Sweden and Poland-Lithuania fought over Livonia; the Polish victory at <b>Kircholm</b> in 1605 delayed Swedish conquest, but Gustav II Adolf took Riga in 1621.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-livonia">Duchy of Livonia</a></li></ul></section>
